Most applications use `driver.Session` and a transport. The modules below
matter when you need to host many sessions, supply an event loop, add a codec,
or drive the protocol without I/O.
[Architecture](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
explains how they fit together.

# Choose the right level

| Name | Job |
| --- | --- |
| **Peer** | The caller-owned engine handle. You feed inbound frames, call `step`, drain outbound frames, and read events. It opens no sockets. |
| **Host** | Manages one identity across many CapTP sessions. It routes introductions between sessions and settles the promises callers wait on. Its socket-free core lives in `driver/core`. The browser package and C embedders that own their I/O drive the Host themselves. |
| **Session** | The one-partner application object: `connect` / `listen`, `fetch`, blocking `call`. It is a view onto a conversation its Host owns. |
| **Server** | The listening side. It publishes objects, and each `accept` returns a `Session` for one partner that connected. |
| **HostRuntime** | The loop behind `Session` and `Server`. One thread serves every session of one Host, with one identity, over the transports you register. |
| **HostTurn** | The argument passed to an object's `handle` method. It carries the turn's arena for building replies and the Host for calling other objects. |

Use `Session` unless you need a lower-level responsibility from the table.
Every `Session` is backed by a Host, which redeems introductions and returns
a live capability. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains three-party
introductions.

# The protocol core

The core is synchronous. It holds no sockets and starts no background work, so
you can test it without a host runtime.

- **foundation** owns shared primitives: a `Frame` owns bytes, a `FrameView`
  borrows them, and IDs are plain values.
- **model** is the CapTP vocabulary, independent of the bytes that carried it.
- **codec** turns model values into wire bytes and back.
  [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) is what Goblins speaks.
  [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second encoding.
  [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) compares them.
- **session** owns the import, export, and answer tables and decides what is
  valid. A codec never decides whether a reference is allowed.
- **engine** advances the protocol state machine and produces events and outbound frames.

# Above the core

- **actors** provides a `Vat`, a container of objects shaped like Goblins'.
  `$` calls an object in the same vat immediately. `<-` sends to one that may
  be remote and returns a promise. It adds nothing to the protocol that the
  engine lacks.
- **driver/core** serves many sessions at once, with no sockets of its own.
  A build check keeps I/O out of it.
- **net** is where `std.Io` enters. It holds the transports:
  [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) QUIC, `noise-tcp`,
  [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')),
  [onion]($link.page('transports/onion')), and the cleartext `tcp`,
  `keyed-tcp`, and `inproc`, among others
  ([Transports]($link.page('transports'))).
- **loam** is optional storage for objects and the capabilities they hold.
  Its core does no I/O. An optional SQLite or LMDB module supplies the disk.
  [Loam]($link.page('loam')) explains records, sleepy actors, and durable
  names.

> **Vat**: one single-threaded container of objects, the unit
> [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and the wider ocap
> world organize around. Every call into a vat runs as one *turn*, start to
> finish, before the next begins.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)**: from
> [E](https://web.archive.org/web/20241231230559/http://erights.org/). Near runs immediately in your vat. Eventual queues a later
> turn and returns a promise. That is also the only way to call across a
> session.

# Why Zig

glowcap's Zig APIs take explicit allocators, so applications control how
memory is allocated and released.
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) publishes what glowcap costs.

glowcap tests memory handling with leak checks, allocation budgets, and
fuzzing of input from peers. These checks do not prove memory safety.

# Public entry points

[Choose an API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares the entry points available to
applications.

- **c_abi** is the C interface: opaque handles, fixed-width return codes,
  `ptr + len` byte views, and explicit release functions. `libglowcap_host` is the CapTP host the bindings wrap. The Rust,
  Python, TypeScript, and Racket bindings also load `libglowcap_driver` to run the loop.
- **driver** is Zig above `driver/core`: `Session`, `Server`, and
  `HostRuntime`.

[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) provide the application API in Rust,
TypeScript, Python, and Racket, each over the C ABI.
